  <page>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>X</m> be a non-empty set and let <m>\mu^*</m> be an outer-measure on
                    <m>X</m>.
                    Take a subset <m>E \subseteq X</m>.
                    Show that, if <m>\mu^*(E) = 0</m>, then <m>E</m> is <m>\mu^*</m>-measurable.
                </p>            
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Assume <m>\mu^*(E)=0</m>.
                Let <m>A\subseteq X</m> be any subset.
                Since <m>A\cap E</m>, we have that <m>\mu^*(A\cap E)=0</m> by the monotonicity of <m>\mu^*</m>.
                We also have that, <m>\mu^*(A)\geq \mu^*(A\cap E^c)</m>, again by monotonicity.
                Thus,
                <md>
                    <mrow>\mu^*(A) \amp = \mu^*(A) + \mu^*(A\cap E)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp \geq \mu^*(A\cap E^c)+ \mu^*(A\cap E).</mrow>
                </md>
                It follows that <m>\mu^*(A) = \mu^*(A\cap E) + \mu^*(A\cap E^c)</m>, and hence <m>E</m> is <m>\mu^*</m>-measurable.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>X</m> be an uncountable set.
                Let <m>\S</m> be the collection of all countable subsets of <m>X</m>.
                Define <m>\nu \colon \S \rightarrow [0,\infty]</m>, by <m>\nu(A) = 0</m> for
                all <m>A \in \S</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that <m>\S</m> is a semiring, and that <m>\nu</m> is a premeasure on <m>\S</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Any subset of a countable set is countable, <url href="https://adamfullermath.github.io/graduate-analysis/appendix-prereqs-4.html#prop-countable">Proposition A.2.6.</url>.
                    Thus, is <m>A,B \in \S</m> then <m>A \cap B \in \S</m> and <m>A\backslash B \in \S</m>.
                    Hence <m>\S</m> is a semiring.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If <m>\{A_i\}_{i=1}^n</m> are disjoint countable sets then
                    <md>
                        0 = \nu\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i\right) = \sum_{i=1}^n 0 = \sum_{i=1}^n\nu(A_i).
                    </md>
                    And if <m>A \subseteq \bigcup_{i=1}^\infty A_i</m> for a countable family <m>\{A_i\}_{i=1}^\infty</m> of countable sets, then
                    <md>
                        0 = \nu(A) = \sum_{i=1}^n 0 = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \nu(A_i).
                    </md>
                    Hence <m>\nu</m> is a premeasure on <m>\S.</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>\nu^*</m> be the outer measure induced by <m>\nu</m>.
                    Calculate <m>\nu^*(A)</m> for each <m>A \subseteq X</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>A\subseteq X</m> be countable.
                    Then <m>\mu^*(A)=0</m>.
                    This can be shown directly.
                    Alternatively, it follows from <url href="https://adamfullermath.github.io/graduate-analysis/sec-construction.html#thm-Car-ext-1">Theorem 2.3.9</url>.

                </p>

                <p>
                    Suppose now that <m>A\subseteq X</m> is uncountable.
                    Since a countable union of countable sets is countable, there is no countable collection of sets <m>\{A_i\}_{i=1}^\infty</m> in <m>\S</m> such that <m>A \subseteq \bigcup_{i=1}^\infty A_i</m>.
                    Thus <m>\mu^*(A) = \infty</m>, (see <url href="https://adamfullermath.github.io/graduate-analysis/sec-construction.html#thm-outer-measure">Theorem 2.3.2</url>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Hence, for <m>A \subseteq X</m>
                    <md>
                        \nu^*(A) = \begin{cases}0 \amp A \text{ countable},\\
                        \infty \amp A \text{ uncountable}.\end{cases}
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>\N</m> be the collection of <m>\nu^*</m>-measurable sets.
                    Give a description of the <m>\nu^*</m>-measurable subsets.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We will show that <m>\N = \P(X)</m>.
                    To this end, fix <m>E \subseteq X</m>.
                    Take any <m>A \subseteq X</m>.
                    If <m>A</m> is countable, then both <m>A\cap E</m> and <m>A \cap E^c</m> are countable.
                    Thus,
                    <m>\nu^*(A)=\nu^*(A\cap E) + \nu^*(A\cap E^c)=0.</m>
                    If <m>A</m> is uncountable, then at least one of <m>A \cap E</m> and <m>A \cap E^c</m> is uncountable.
                    Thus,
                    <md>
                        \nu^*(A) = \infty = \nu^*(A\cap E) + \nu^*(A\cap E^c).
                    </md>
                    Hence <m>E</m> is measurable, and thus <m>\N = \P(X).</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Define <m>\mu \colon \P(X) \rightarrow [0,\infty]</m> by
                    <m>\mu(A) = 0</m> for all <m>A \subseteq X</m>.
                    Show that <m>\mu</m> is a measure on <m>(X, \P(X))</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    There isn't much to show here. <m>\mu(\emptyset) = 0</m>, and
                    <md>
                        \mu\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^\infty A_i\right) = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \mu(A_i)=0
                    </md>
                    for any countable collection of sets <m>\{A_i\}_{i=1}^\infty</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>\overline{\nu}</m> be the Carathéodory extension of <m>\nu</m> (i.e. <m>\overline{\nu}</m> is the restriction of <m>\nu^*</m> to the <m>\nu^*</m>-measurable sets).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Note that <m>\mu</m> is also an extension of <m>\S</m>.
                    Why does this not contradict the Carathéodory-Hahn Theorem?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The Carathéodory-Hahn Theorem only guarantees uniqueness in the case when the premeasure <m>\nu</m> is <m>\sigma</m>-finite.
                    As <m>X</m> is uncountable, and <m>\S</m> is the collection of all countable subsets of <m>X</m>, <m>X</m> can not be covered by countably many elements of <m>\S</m>.
                    Thus <m>\nu</m> is not <m>\sigma</m>-finite.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>C \subseteq [0,1]</m> be the Cantor set; let
                <m>U = [0,1]\backslash C</m>; and let
                <m>\phi \colon [0,1] \rightarrow [0,1]</m> be the Cantor-Lebesgue function.
                Let <m>\mu</m> be the Borel measure on <m>[0,1]</m> induced by <m>\phi</m>.
                I.e. <m>\mu((c,d]) = \phi(d) - \phi(c)</m> and <m>\mu(\{0\}) = \phi(0).</m>
            </p>

            <p>
                Show that <m>\mu([0,1]) = 1</m>, <m>\mu(U) = 0</m>, and <m>\mu(C) = 1</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                By the definition of <m>\phi</m> (and <m>U</m>), we can write <m>U = \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty I_k</m>, where <m>\{I_k\}_{k=1}^\infty</m> is a disjoint collection of open intervals, and <m>\phi</m> is constant on each <m>I_k.</m>
                For each <m>k</m>, let <m>I_k= (a_k,b_k).</m>
            </p>

            <p>
                Fix <m>k</m>.
                As <m>\phi</m> is continuous, and <m>\phi(x)</m> is constant for all <m>x \in (a_k,b_k)</m>, it follows that <m>\phi</m> is constant on <m>[a_k,b_k]</m>.
                Hence
                <md>
                    \mu(I_k) \leq \mu((a_k,b_k]) = \phi(b_k) - \phi(a_k) = 0.
                </md>
                Thus, <m>\mu(I_k)=0.</m>
                Therefore,
                <md>
                    \mu(U)= \sum_{k=1}^\infty \mu(I_k) = 0.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>\phi</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\mu</m> it follows that
                <md>
                    \mu([0,1])= \phi(1).
                </md>
                By the definition of <m>\phi</m>,
                <md>
                    \phi(1) = \sup_n\left\{\frac{2^n-1}{2^n} \right\} =1.
                </md>
                Hence, <m>\mu([0,1])=1</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Finally, since <m>C \cup U = [0,1]</m>, it follows from the excision property that
                <md>\mu(C) = \mu([0,1])-\mu(U)=1.</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose <m>\mu</m> and <m>\nu</m> are measures on a measurable space <m>(X,\mathcal{F})</m> and <m>\mu \geq \nu</m> (i.e. <m>\mu(E) \geq \nu(E)</m> for all <m>E\in \mathcal{F}</m>).
            Define <m>\lambda</m> on <m>\mathcal{F}</m> by
            <md> \lambda(E) = \sup\{\mu(F) - \nu(F) \colon F \subseteq E \text{ measurable, } \nu(F)&lt; \infty\}. </md>        
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that <m>\lambda</m> is a measure on <m>(X,\mathcal{F})</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                First note that we can't define <m>\lambda(E) = \mu(E) - \nu(E)</m> since this value may not be defined (we could be looking at <m>\infty - \infty</m>).
            </p>
            <p>
                It is clear that <m>\lambda(\emptyset)=0.</m>
                Let <m>\{E_k\}_{k=1}^\infty</m> be disjoint sets in <m>\F</m> and let <m>E = \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty E_k</m>.
                Note that if <m>F \subseteq E</m> is measurable with <m>\nu(F)\lt \infty</m>, then <m>\{F \cap E_k\}_k</m> is a disjoint collection of measurable sets with <m>F\cap E_k\subseteq E_k</m> and <m>\nu(F\cap E_k)\lt\infty.</m>
                However, if <m>\{F_k\}_k</m> is a collection of (necessarily disjoint) sets with <m>F_k \subseteq E_k</m> and <m>\nu(F_k)\lt \infty</m> for each <m>k</m>, then it is possible that <m>\nu\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty F_k\right)=\infty.</m>
                Hence,
                <md>
                    <mrow>\lambda(E) \amp = \sup \left\{\mu(F)-\nu(F) \colon F\subseteq E,\ \nu(F)\lt \infty \right\} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \sup \left\{\mu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right)-\nu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right) \colon F_k\subseteq E_k,\ \nu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right)\lt \infty \right\} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp \leq  \sup \left\{\mu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right)-\nu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right) \colon F_k\subseteq E_k,\ \nu(F_k)\lt \infty \right\} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp =  \sup \left\{\sum_{k=1}^\infty[\mu(F_k)-\nu(F_k)] \colon F_k\subseteq E_k,\ \nu(F_k)\lt \infty \right\} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp =  \sum_{k=1}^\infty\sup \left\{\mu(F_k)-\nu(F_k) \colon F_k\subseteq E_k,\ \nu(F_k)\lt \infty \right\}</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \lambda(E_k). </mrow>
                </md>
                Note that the penultimate inequality holds since the <m>\{F_k\}_{k=1}^\infty</m> can be chosen independently of eachother, and all terms are positive (in general the supremum of a sum is less than or equal to the sum of the suprema).
            </p>
            <p>
                Fix <m>n</m>.
                Note that, a measurable set <m>F \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^n E_k</m> satisfies <m>\nu(F) \lt \infty</m> if and only if <m>\nu(F\cap E_k)\lt \infty</m> for each <m>k=1,\ldots,n.</m>
                Hence, by similar reasoning as above,
                <md>
                    \lambda\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^n E_k\right) = \sum_{k=1}^n \lambda(E_k).
                </md>
                Thus,
                <md>
                    <mrow>\lambda(E) \amp = \sup \left\{\mu(F)-\nu(F) \colon F\subseteq E,\ \nu(F)\lt \infty \right\} </mrow> 
                    <mrow> \amp= \left\{\mu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right)-\nu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right) \colon F_k\subseteq E_k,\ \nu\left(\bigcup_k F_k\right)\lt \infty \right\} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp \geq \left\{\mu\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^n F_k\right)-\nu\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^n F_k\right) \colon F_k\subseteq E_k,\ \nu(F_k)\lt \infty \right\}  </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp =  \sum_{k=1}^n \lambda(E_k). </mrow>
                </md>
                As this holds for all <m>n</m>, it follows that
                <md>
                    \lambda(E) \geq \sum_{k=1}^\infty \lambda(E_k).
                </md>
                We have thus shown that <m>\lambda(E) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty\lambda(E_k)</m>, and hence <m>\lambda</m> is a measure.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that <m>\nu + \lambda = \mu</m> (where <m>\nu + \lambda</m> is defined by <m>(\nu+\lambda)(E) = \nu(E) + \lambda(E)</m> for <m>E \in \F</m>).
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Take <m>E \in \F</m>.
                If <m>\nu(E) = \infty</m>, then <m>\mu(E)=\infty</m> and hence
                <md>
                    \infty = \lambda(E) + \nu(E) = \mu(E).
                </md>
                Suppose now that <m>\nu(E)\lt \infty.</m>
                Then for all measurable <m>F \subseteq E</m>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\mu(E) - \nu(E) \amp= (\mu(F) - \nu(F)) + (\mu(E\backslash F)-\nu(E\backslash F)) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp\geq \mu(F) - \nu(F).  </mrow>
                </md>
                Thus <m>\lambda(E) = \mu(E) - \nu(E)</m>, and so <m>\lambda(E) + \nu(E) = \mu(E).</m>             
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="sig-fin">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that if <m>(X,\mathcal{F},\nu)</m> is <m>\sigma</m>-finite then the <m>\lambda</m> is the unique measure satisfying <m>\nu + \lambda = \mu</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Assume there are two such measures <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> and show they have to be equal on sets with <m>\nu(E)&lt;\infty</m>. Then move to general measurable sets.
            </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Let <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> be two measures satisfying
                <md>
                    \nu + \lambda_1 = \mu =\nu + \lambda_2.
                </md>
                Take <m>F\subseteq X</m> measurable with <m>\nu(F)\lt \infty.</m>
                Then
                <md>
                    \nu(F) + \lambda_1(F) = \nu(F) + \lambda_2(F).
                </md>
                As <m>\nu(F)\lt\infty</m>, it follows that <m>\lambda_1(F) = \lambda_2(F).</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                If <m>(X,\F,\nu)</m> is <m>\sigma</m>-finite, then there are disjoint measurable sets <m>\{E_k\}_k</m> sets with <m>X = \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty E_k</m> and <m>\nu(E_k)\lt\infty</m> for each <m>k</m>.
                Take any measurable <m>F \subseteq X</m>.
                Then, for each <m>k</m>, <m>\nu(F\cap E_k)\lt \infty</m>, and so
                <md>
                    <mrow>\lambda_1(F)  \amp= \sum_{k=1}^\infty \lambda_1(F\cap E_k) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp= \sum_{k=1}^\infty \lambda_2(F\cap E_k) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp= \lambda_2(F). </mrow>
                </md>
                Hence <m>\lambda_1 = \lambda_2.</m>
            </p>
            <p>
            Note that, if <m>(X,\F,\nu)</m> is not <m>\sigma</m>-finite, then <xref ref="sig-fin"/> does not necessarily hold.
                For example, let <m>\nu</m> be the measure defined by
                <md>\nu(E) = \begin{cases}0 &amp; \text{if }E = \emptyset\\ \infty &amp; \text{if }E \neq \emptyset,\end{cases} </md>
                and let <m>\mu = \nu</m>.
                Then, if <m>\lambda</m> is <em>any</em> measure on <m>(X,\F)</m>, we have that
                <md>
                    \nu + \lambda = \mu.
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
